   "title": "Media association challenges privacy law in Supreme Court",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/indian-publishers-contest-modi-governments-privacy-law/article71627054.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Tue, 27 Oct 2026 09:50:23 +0530",
   "_dt": 1793074823.0,
   "ageDays": 0.07,
   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription A group of 21 news publishers has challenged the Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) Act, 2023 , India’s privacy law, in court, arguing it would trample investigative work and let the government identify journalists’ sources, documents show.",
   "summary_zh": "这些内容可通过订阅解锁。一家由21家新闻出版机构组成的团体已就印度2023年《数字个人数据保护法》向法院提起诉讼。文件显示，该团体认为，这项隐私法将压制调查工作，并使政府能够识别记者的消息来源。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription A group of 21 news publishers has challenged the Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) Act, 2023 , India’s privacy law, in court, arguing it would trample investigative work and let the government identify journalists’ sources, documents show. Also read | Supreme Court to examine impact of data protection law on RTI, investigative journalism Prime Minister Narendra Modi’s government announced the implementation of the law in 2025 to protect the digital personal data of users, raising compliance requirements for platforms like Meta and Google. The publishers have said that since media outlets were not exempt, the law was an invasion of press freedom. The October 6, 2026 lawsuit, being reported for the first time and due to be heard on Tuesday (October 27, 2026), is the most high-profile challenge to the government’s privacy law. Until now, only free speech activists and a group of smaller news outlets had challenged it. It also marks an unusual challenge to the government from businesses headed by Mr. Ambani and Mr. Adani, who Opposition parties allege are close to Mr. Modi and get favourable treatment — charges the businesses and the government vehemently deny. DNPA and the media groups did not respond to requests for comment from Reuters , and neither did the IT Ministry. The publishers are demanding that most of the law’s contentious provisions, which kick in from May 2027, be quashed. They are arguing that the law could force a journalist to get prior consent from a person they want to investigate and force publishers to erase stories. They also say powers granted by the law to demand information could expose reporters’ sources. The law would “render investigative journalism a benign and neutered recorder of events,” the 698-page filing said. The provisions “constitute pre-censorship which is anathema to any democracy worth its name”. The lawsuit comes amid accusations of declining press freedom in India since Mr. Modi took office in 2014. Critics say many mainstream media outlets have not done enough to scrutinise the Modi government. India’s ranking in Reporters Without Borders’ World Press Freedom Index fell to 157 th out of 180 countries this year, from 140 th in 2014 — but the government rejects the rankings. Modi government says it respects press freedom and says India, which has a “vibrant press and media ecosystem” of hundreds of newspapers and TV channels, does not need validation from foreign bodies. DNPA’s members include Mr. Ambani’s Network18 , which has seven digital news platforms, Mr. Adani’s NDTV and other marquee media groups, including the Indian Express and Times Internet , which is part of the Times of India group, publisher of India’s largest-selling English-language daily. A key concern in the law is the requirement of consent, the group’s filing says. The law also lets people withdraw consent at any time and demand that their data be erased, without any public-interest safeguard, which DNPA says could force its members to unpublish or heavily redact a factual news report. News publishers are concerned that a federal authority created under the law can impose fines for non-compliance, and grants powers to New Delhi to block access to any information in the “interests of the general public”, which would curtail press freedom, DNPA said. In an August letter to the government attached to the lawsuit, the group said India “has chosen to grant broad exemptions to the state, while failing to extend any corresponding protection to the press”. No response was cited. From next year, the law will also let New Delhi order any organization handling personal data “to furnish such information as it may call for”, putting newsrooms at risk of having to divulge sources, the filing said. It can also deter whistleblowers from sharing information on corruption, corporate wrongdoing or electoral malpractice with reporters, the DNPA argued. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "in"
   ],
   "topic": [
    "pol"
   ],
   "tier": 4,
   "actor": "第三方",
   "alsoUS": false,
   "cnHit": false,
